I just meant in the order of the book.  It's great
that there's a reference to Bayesian kappa coefficient
estimates --- I've been suggesting to people that they
do that for years.

I work on data annotation/rating/coding tasks, so
evaluating rater agreement is a problem near and dear
to my heart.  There are some really nifty Bayesian
models in the area (Girolami and crew have one for ordinals
with Gaussian process priors; Raykar et al. have one that
includes an automatically trained system as a rater.

It has some psychological relevance because it's all
about humans making categorical judgments and their
accuracies and biases --- so it's also related to education
models like IRT if you start thinking about things like
item difficulty.

- Bob

