Il 17/09/2013 02:24, Ben Goodrich ha scritto:
> On Monday, September 16, 2013 6:52:04 PM UTC-4, Bob Carpenter wrote:
>
>      > But an explicit LKJ (lkj_corr or lkj_corr_cholesky) would be a
>     no-op / waste of time.
>
>     No.  It's just the lkj_corr(1) prior that's a no-op, because
>
>        lkj_corr(Sigma|nu) = determinant(Sigma)^{nu - 1}.
>
>     So when nu == 1, the result is a constant, hence a uniform prior.
>     You already get that from the declaration, so it's essentially
>     a no-op.
>
>
> I think saying it has no effect on the posterior distribution would be
> clearer than saying it is a no-op. If nu == 1, then
>
> lkj_corr = ifelse(Sigma is a correlation matrix, 1, 0)
>
> but verifying whether Sigma is, in fact, a correlation matrix is O(K^3).
> So, it is essentially no-op after doing an expensive operation.
>
> Thus, omitting the
>
> |
> Sigma~lkj_corr(1.0)
> |
>
> does save a lot of time for large K.
>
> Ben
