Hi Dan, thanks for these comments,

That makes more sense about the value of fc_all (i.e. being NAN).  I will hunt around and see if I can find anything uninitialized, though the model still runs most of the time, it just seems that 1 out of 20 iterations falls apart. This should rule out something being uninitialized, correct?

You are correct that some parameters set set and reset a number of times. The model I am trying to construct is a Markov model, so there is a vector that gets updated many times.

For the variables that get updated many times over, maybe I should be initializing them as local variables inside the loop, and I will give that a try.

I definitely agree with starting simple -- I have been trying to do so but this is the moment where things have started to break down. Sorry the model looks like a condstruction zone, if I need to send out for further advice it will definitely be a cleaned up verison.

Frankly, my big worry is around the autodiff: this model will do a lot of manipulation within the transformed parameters block, such that the values that will finally be used in the likelihood will be a <very> complex function of the model parameters. Is this something that might boggle the gradient calculations?  This has been my concern all along but things have (seemed to have) been working so far.  I saw Bob's post on Andres'w blog the other day on 'samplers for big science', and though this model will be a teeny-tiny thing compared to some of them, it might be heading in that direction.

Again, thanks for the advice.

Nick

