Il 25/09/2013 19:53, Bob Carpenter ha scritto:
> With theta^y, it's not clear what to do with 0^0.  R defines
> the value to be 1, so log(0^0) = 1.

A very nice question. R is in good company:
http://www.math.hmc.edu/funfacts/ffiles/10005.3-5.shtml
http://www.askamathematician.com/2010/12/q-what-does-00-zero-raised-to-the-zeroth-power-equal-why-do-mathematicians-and-high-school-teachers-disagree/
http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exponentiation#Zero_to_the_power_of_zero

In any case, if 0^0 = 1 then log(0^0) = 0 :-)

Sergio

