Right, this is what I think was Andrew's motivation behind having an upper bound.  Theoretically you take full advantage of
HMC when you run until you turn around -- anything less drops your performance, especially in high dimensions.  But if
it takes more than 2^{10} steps to cross the posterior your model is crazy and the folk theorem comes into play.  Usually some
care in the choice of priors (and make sure all constraints are explicitly taken into account in your data types) makes a huge difference.

On Sep 27, 2013, at 6:54 PM, Ross Boylan <ro...@biostat.ucsf.edu> wrote:

> Another way of interpreting this is that if estimation hits a depth of
> 10 it is a clue to reconsider your model.  My initial efforts hit the
> bound.  Although the usual advice is to simplify, adding a parameter
> was what solved the problem in my case.  I had fixed, diffuse
> priors, and the fix was to make the dispersion of the priors a
> hierarchical parameter.

From uedd4914c326b6d31 at groups.google.com  Fri Sep 27 20:59:21 2013
